        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Turning on a treadmill should take ten seconds, and usually does. The reason people search
          for it is that the first attempt did nothing: the screen stayed dark, or it lit up and
          the belt would not move. Almost always, one step in a short sequence was missed — the
          switch at the base was off, the safety key was not seated, the console was still
          booting, or the machine was plugged into a power strip that cannot cope. This page sets
          out that sequence in order, explains the parts people cannot find, adds the brand
          notes that can be stated with confidence, and points to the right fault guide if the
          machine still will not wake.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. How Do You Turn On a Treadmill? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Plug the treadmill directly into a wall outlet, switch on the main power switch
            (usually on the front of the base near where the cord enters the frame), make sure the
            safety key is in place on the console, wait for the console to start up, then press
            the start or quick-start control.</strong> The belt begins at a slow speed and you
            adjust from there.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Plug in at the wall.</strong> Not through a power strip or extension cord
            unless your manual specifically allows it.</li>
            <li><strong>Turn on the power switch.</strong> It is usually a rocker switch at the
            front of the motor end, close to the power cord, rather than on the console.</li>
            <li><strong>Fit the safety key.</strong> Without it, many treadmills will not power the
            console fully, or will not let the belt start.</li>
            <li><strong>Let the console start.</strong> A simple LED display lights at once; a
            touchscreen can take a minute or more, longer if it is updating.</li>
            <li><strong>Press start.</strong> On touchscreen machines you may need to choose a
            manual workout or a user profile first.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            If the console stays dark after all five steps, the cause is usually one of a short
            list — the key, a tripped reset switch on the machine, the outlet or breaker, the cord
            — and our guide to a
            <a href="/treadmill-wont-turn-on/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill that will not turn on</a>
            works through them in order. If the console lights but the belt will not move, that is
            a different problem with a different set of causes, covered further down.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="plug-in" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Where Should a Treadmill Be Plugged In?</h2>
<p>
            Directly into a grounded wall outlet, on a circuit that meets the requirements in your
            manual. This is the first step and the one most often done wrong, because the natural
            thing to do with a new appliance is plug it into the nearest power strip.
          </p>
          <p>
            A motorised treadmill is one of the larger electrical loads in a home. The motor draws a
            surge of current when the belt starts and whenever load increases — a footstrike, an
            incline change — and that current has to come through every connection between the
            panel and the motor. A power strip, a multi-way adapter or a long, thin extension cord
            adds resistance and heat. At best the machine behaves erratically; at worst the strip
            overheats or trips. Many manufacturers' manuals say plainly not to use an extension
            cord, and some specify a dedicated circuit. Check what yours says, and whether it
            recommends or prohibits a surge protector, which manuals differ on.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Checks worth doing at the outlet</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Is the outlet live?</strong> Plug in a lamp. Outlets controlled by a wall
            switch catch people out, especially in basements and spare rooms.</li>
            <li><strong>Is it a GFCI outlet that has tripped?</strong> Bathrooms, garages,
            basements and outdoor outlets often have GFCI protection, sometimes on a different
            outlet upstream. Press the reset button.</li>
            <li><strong>Is the plug fully seated?</strong> Treadmill cords are heavy and can work
            loose, particularly if the cord runs under tension.</li>
            <li><strong>Is the cord routed safely?</strong> Not under the deck, not across a
            walkway, and not pinched by the folding mechanism.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If the treadmill trips the breaker when it starts, rather than simply staying off, the
            fault-finding is different: it points to the circuit or to a machine drawing too much
            current, and it has its own guide in our fault diagnosis section. Never fit a larger breaker or fuse to stop the trips; the breaker protects
            the wiring in the wall.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-switch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Where Is the Power Switch on a Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            On most motorised home treadmills the main power switch is at the front of the base,
            on the motor end, close to where the power cord enters the frame. It is typically a
            rocker switch marked with the usual on and off symbols, and it sits low down, which is
            why so many owners do not know it exists. It is often not on the console at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            Look at the front of the machine at floor level, on the side where the cord comes out.
            If there is a plastic motor hood, the switch is usually set into it or into the frame
            just below it. On some machines it is on the side of the base rather than the front.
            If you cannot see it, the parts diagram near the front of your manual will show it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The reset button or circuit breaker next to it</h3>
          <p>
            Many treadmills also have a small reset button or built-in circuit breaker beside the
            power switch. It is a protective device for the machine: if the motor draws too much
            current, it trips and cuts power, and the treadmill stays off until it is pressed back
            in. Not every model has one, and on models that do it can look like a small black or
            white button that is easy to mistake for part of the switch. If your machine has one
            and it has popped out, press it in firmly, then try the power switch again.
          </p>
          <p>
            If it trips again soon after, do not keep resetting it. Repeated trips mean something is
            drawing too much current — a dry deck, a tight belt, a motor or controller fault — and
            the fault guides linked below are the next step.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Walking pads and machines with no visible switch</h3>
          <p>
            Walking pads and some compact treadmills vary much more. Some have a switch on the
            side or rear of the base; some power on as soon as they are plugged in and wait in
            standby for a remote control or an app; some have a single button that does both.
            With these machines in particular, the quick-start guide that came in the box is the
            fastest answer, and it is worth keeping somewhere you can find it, because the remote
            and app arrangements differ more between walking-pad brands than switch positions do
            between full treadmills.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety-key" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Does the Safety Key Have to Be In Before It Will Turn On?</h2>
<p>
            On many treadmills, yes. The safety key — a magnetic or plug-in key on a cord with a
            clip at the other end — sits in a slot on the console, and when it is removed the
            machine stops the belt. Depending on the design, a treadmill without its key may show
            nothing at all, may light the console but refuse to start, or may display a message
            asking for the key. All three are normal behaviour, not faults.
          </p>
          <p>
            The key works as an emergency stop. You clip the cord to your clothing at the waist;
            if you fall or drift too far back, the key pulls out and the belt stops. That is why
            the treadmill cares so much about it, and why a missing or badly seated key is one of
            the commonest reasons a perfectly good treadmill "will not turn on".
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Getting the key right</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Seat it fully.</strong> Push it in until it sits flush. Some keys only fit
            one way round; try turning it over.</li>
            <li><strong>Use the right key.</strong> Keys are not universal. A key from another
            machine, or a generic magnetic key, may not trigger the switch.</li>
            <li><strong>Clip it to yourself, not the handrail.</strong> A key clipped to the frame
            cannot do its job.</li>
            <li><strong>Remove it after use</strong> if there are children or pets in the house,
            and keep it out of reach.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If the key is in place and the machine still behaves as if it is missing, or if the key
            has been lost, our guide to a
            <a href="/treadmill-safety-key-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety key that is not working</a>
            covers cleaning the slot, worn keys, the switch behind it and ordering the right
            replacement. Do not bypass the switch with tape or a loose magnet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="console-boot" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How Long Does a Treadmill Console Take to Turn On?</h2>
<p>
            A simple LED or LCD console usually lights within a second or two of the power switch
            going on. A touchscreen console can take considerably longer — commonly somewhere from
            a few seconds to a minute or more — because it is a small computer that has to boot an
            operating system, connect to Wi-Fi and, on connected machines, check in with the
            fitness platform.
          </p>
          <p>
            During that time the screen may show a logo, a progress indicator or nothing much at
            all, and pressing buttons does little. Give it time before deciding something is
            wrong. A touchscreen that seems frozen on a logo for a long time may be installing a
            software update, which can take a good while on a slow connection.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Do not switch off during an update</h3>
          <p>
            If the console indicates an update is in progress, leave it alone until it finishes.
            Cutting power partway through a firmware or software update is one of the few ways an
            owner can create a genuine software fault on a treadmill console. If an update seems
            stuck for a very long time, check the manufacturer's support guidance for your model
            before power-cycling.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Profiles, sign-ins and manual mode</h3>
          <p>
            Connected treadmills often start on a home or profile screen rather than a ready-to-run
            screen. You may need to choose a user, sign in, or select a manual or "just run" style
            workout before the start control appears. The exact wording varies by brand and by
            console software version, and it changes with updates, so if the start control is not
            where you expect, look for a manual workout option on the home screen. Most connected
            machines can be used in a manual mode without a class playing; whether that requires
            an active subscription varies by brand and has changed over time, so check the current
            terms for your machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the console powers up but freezes, reboots itself or goes blank partway through,
            that is a console problem rather than a power problem: a loose console cable, a
            software fault or a failing console board, rather than anything to do with the switch
            or the outlet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="start-the-belt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. How Do You Start the Belt Safely Once It Is On?</h2>
<p>
            Stand on the side rails, not on the belt, clip the safety key to your clothing, then
            press start. Most treadmills give a short countdown and then move the belt at a slow
            walking speed. Step on once the belt is moving steadily, holding the handrails for
            balance as you do, then let go and adjust the speed.
          </p>
          <p>
            The control to begin is usually labelled start, quick start, go or something similar,
            often in green. On many machines quick start skips the programme menus and begins a
            manual session straight away. On a walking pad it may be a button on a remote control
            or in an app. If the control is a touchscreen icon rather than a physical button, it
            may only appear once the console has finished starting and you have chosen a workout.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A few habits worth starting with</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Start slowly.</strong> Let the belt reach a walking pace and walk for a few
            minutes before raising the speed.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the incline is at zero</strong> before starting, especially if
            someone else used the machine last.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep the area behind the treadmill clear.</strong> Manuals typically specify
            clearance behind the deck, because that is where a fall ends up.</li>
            <li><strong>Know where stop is.</strong> Find the stop or pause control before you need
            it, and remember that pulling the safety key also stops the belt.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If the console is lit and accepts the start command but the belt does not move, or
            moves and stops, that is not a turning-on problem. The causes — the key switch, the
            drive belt, the motor, the controller, a speed sensor — are covered in our guide to a
            <a href="/treadmill-turns-on-but-belt-wont-move/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill that turns on but the belt will not move</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="brand-notes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. How Do You Turn On a NordicTrack, ProForm or Peloton Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            The same general sequence applies, and the honest answer for any brand is that the
            manual for your model number is the authority. Brand ranges change, console generations
            change, and the button and menu names that applied to one model often do not apply to
            the next. What follows are only the points that are widely consistent.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">NordicTrack and ProForm</h3>
          <p>
            On many NordicTrack and ProForm treadmills, the power switch is at the front of the
            base near where the power cord enters the frame, not on the console, and the safety
            key must be in its slot on the console before the treadmill will run. Many models
            also have a reset or circuit breaker button near the power switch. Touchscreen models
            in these ranges are connected consoles that need time to boot, and may start on a
            profile or home screen; look for a manual workout option if you want to start without
            a class.
          </p>
          <p>
            Beyond that, layouts vary by model and year, so find the model number (usually on a
            label on the frame) and use the manual for that exact model. Our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-reset-nordictrack-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to reset a NordicTrack treadmill</a>
            covers the reset switch, power cycling and the console reset in detail, and our
            <a href="/proform-treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm treadmill troubleshooting</a>
            guide does the same for ProForm, including consoles that look dead because they are
            asleep.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Peloton</h3>
          <p>
            Peloton treadmills follow the same broad pattern: a wall outlet, a power switch on the
            machine, a safety key, and a touchscreen that has to boot before you can start. Where
            exactly the switch sits and how the start controls work differs between Peloton
            models, so use Peloton's own setup guidance and manual for your model rather than
            general instructions. Peloton treadmills have been the subject of safety recalls in
            the past; if you own one, it is worth checking the manufacturer's current safety
            information for your model and following any instructions it gives.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Other brands and walking pads</h3>
          <p>
            Other treadmill brands, and the many walking-pad brands, generally follow one of the
            two patterns on this page: a base switch, a safety key and a console start
            control, or a plug-in-and-standby design with a remote or app. If in doubt, the
            quick-start sheet in the box is usually more useful than a search result.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sleep-and-off" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Is It Off, Asleep or in Standby? And How Do You Turn It Off?</h2>
<p>
            Many treadmill consoles go to sleep after a period without use, and a sleeping console
            can look exactly like a machine that is switched off. Touching the screen, pressing a
            button or reinserting the safety key usually wakes it. If the power switch at the base
            is on and the console is dark, try waking it before assuming a fault.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some machines also have a standby or low-power mode that keeps the console partly
            alive so it can wake quickly and download updates. In that state the machine is still
            drawing a small amount of power and is still live, which matters if you are about to
            clean it or move it. The standby draw is small, but the machine is not disconnected.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Turning a treadmill off properly</h3>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Stop the belt</strong> with the stop control and let it come to a halt.
            Do not step off a moving belt.</li>
            <li><strong>End or save the workout</strong> on a connected console if you want the
            session recorded.</li>
            <li><strong>Remove the safety key</strong> and keep it somewhere children cannot reach
            it.</li>
            <li><strong>Turn off the power switch</strong> at the base. Many owners leave connected
            machines on so they can update overnight; that is a choice, but switching off at the
            base is the simple way to make sure the machine cannot be started.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Unplug at the wall before any cleaning, lubrication or adjustment, before moving the
            treadmill, during a long absence, and during electrical storms if your manual
            recommends it. Switching off at the base is not the same as being disconnected from
            the supply.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wont-turn-on" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What If the Treadmill Still Will Not Turn On?</h2>
<p>
            Work through the free checks in order before assuming anything has failed. A dead
            treadmill is far more often a key, a switch or an outlet than a broken part.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Safety key</strong> fully seated, the right way round, the right key for
            the machine.</li>
            <li><strong>Power switch</strong> at the base on.</li>
            <li><strong>Reset button or circuit breaker</strong> on the machine pressed in, if it
            has one.</li>
            <li><strong>Outlet</strong> live, GFCI not tripped, house breaker not tripped, and no
            power strip or extension cord in the way.</li>
            <li><strong>Cord</strong> undamaged and fully pushed in at both ends, where it plugs
            into the machine as well as the wall.</li>
            <li><strong>Console asleep or updating</strong> rather than off: touch the screen and
            give it a couple of minutes.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            If those six checks do not bring it to life, the problem is likely to be inside the
            machine — an internal fuse, the console cable, the controller board — and our full
            guide to a
            <a href="/treadmill-wont-turn-on/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill that will not turn on</a>
            takes it from there, including when the repair is worth paying for. Brand-specific
            guides help with NordicTrack and ProForm consoles in particular, because their reset
            routines and console behaviour have their own quirks.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two rules apply throughout. Unplug at the wall before taking any cover off, and do not
            touch the motor control board; capacitors on it can hold a charge after the machine
            has been unplugged. And if the machine is under warranty, contact the manufacturer
            before opening anything beyond what the manual describes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="first-time" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Anything Different the First Time, or After a Move?</h2>
<p>
            Yes, a few checks. A treadmill that has just been assembled, moved or stored is the
            one most likely not to start, and usually for a reason that takes a minute to fix.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>After assembly,</strong> the most common culprit is the console cable. It runs
            up through an upright to a connector at the console, and if it is not fully pushed
            together, or was pinched when the upright was bolted on, the console stays dark. Our
            guide on
            <a href="/how-to-assemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to assemble a treadmill</a>
            covers routing it so this does not happen.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>After a move,</strong> check the same cable, check that nothing was knocked
            loose at the base switch or cord inlet, and make sure the deck is fully unfolded and
            locked; some machines will not run with the deck partly raised. If the console or
            uprights were removed for the move, reconnect exactly as they came apart.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>After cold storage,</strong> such as a garage or a van in winter, let the
            treadmill come up to room temperature before switching it on. Moving a cold machine
            into a warm room can cause condensation inside the console and controller, and power
            should not go into damp electronics. A few hours indoors is a sensible margin, and
            longer if the machine was very cold.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Before the first session,</strong> check the belt is centred and tensioned and
            the deck is lubricated if the manual calls for it at set-up. Then start at a slow walk
            for a few minutes and listen. New machines can make some noise while they settle in,
            but grinding, scraping or a smell of hot electrics means stop and investigate.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you turn on a NordicTrack treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On many NordicTrack treadmills, plug the machine directly into a wall outlet, switch on the power switch at the front of the base near the power cord, put the safety key in its slot on the console, and wait for the console to boot. Then choose a workout or manual mode and press start. Check the manual for your model number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where is the on switch on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On most motorised home treadmills the main power switch is at the front of the base, near where the power cord enters the frame, often low down on or beneath the motor hood. It is usually not on the console. Some walking pads use a side switch, a remote or an app instead. The parts diagram in the manual shows its location.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why will my treadmill not turn on even though it is plugged in?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The most common reasons are a missing or badly seated safety key, the power switch at the base being off, a tripped reset button on the machine, a dead or tripped outlet, or a power strip that cannot carry the load. A sleeping or updating console can also look dead. Work through those before suspecting an internal fault.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a treadmill turn on without the safety key?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the design. Some treadmills show nothing without the key, some light the console but refuse to start the belt, and some display a message asking for it. In all cases the belt should not run without the key in place. If the key is fitted and the machine acts as if it is missing, the key or switch may need attention.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a treadmill touchscreen take to turn on?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Simple LED consoles light almost immediately. Touchscreen consoles boot like a small computer and commonly take from several seconds to a minute or more, longer when installing a software update. Leave the machine alone during an update, because cutting power partway through can cause a genuine software fault on the console.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should you turn off a treadmill after every use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Stopping the belt and removing the safety key after every session is sensible, especially with children in the house. Switching off at the base switch is a good habit, though some owners leave connected machines on so they update overnight. Unplug at the wall before cleaning, adjusting or moving it, and during long absences.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Turning On a Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most treadmills that seem not to turn on are working machines with one step missed. These are the ones that catch people out.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging it into a power strip or extension cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A treadmill motor draws a large current at start-up, and strips and thin cords add resistance and heat. The machine may stay dark, behave erratically or trip something. Plug it directly into a grounded wall outlet that meets the manual's circuit requirements, and check whether the manual allows a surge protector.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Looking for the power switch on the console</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">On most home treadmills the main switch is at floor level on the front of the base, near the power cord. Owners press every console button and conclude the machine is broken. Check the base first, and look for a reset button beside the switch that may have tripped.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting the power during a console update</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A touchscreen that seems frozen on a logo may be updating. Switching it off partway through is one of the few ways to create a real software fault. Give it time, and check the manufacturer's support guidance before power-cycling a console that appears stuck on an update.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Clipping the safety key to the frame</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">People clip the key to the handrail so it stops getting in the way. The machine turns on and runs, but the emergency stop can no longer work, because a fall will not pull the key out. Clip it to your clothing at the waist every session.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

